/-!
# Quasi-regular sequences are stable under flat base change

Let `R → S` be a flat ring map, let `M` be an `R`-module and let `N` be the base change of `M`
to `S`, exhibited by an `R`-linear `bc : M →ₗ[R] N` with `IsBaseChange S bc`. Write
`J = (f_1, …, f_c) ⊆ R` for the ideal generated by a sequence `f_1, …, f_c` of elements of `R`,
`J' = JS` for the ideal generated by their images and `Φ_M` for the canonical map of graded
`(R/J)[X_1, …, X_c]`-modules `(R/J)[X] ⊗_{R/J} (M/JM) → ⨁_{n ≥ 0} JⁿM/J^{n+1}M` of tag 061N.

Flatness makes the whole construction commute with `- ⊗_R S`. On the target, `JⁿM ⊗_R S = (J')ⁿN`
because `0 → JⁿM → M` stays exact, and then `(J')ⁿN/(J')^{n+1}N = (JⁿM/J^{n+1}M) ⊗_R S` because
`0 → J^{n+1}M → JⁿM → JⁿM/J^{n+1}M → 0` does; on the source, `N/J'N = (M/JM) ⊗_R S` and the
monomial basis of `(R/J)[X]` turns the source into a direct sum of copies of `M/JM`. So `Φ_N` is
`Φ_M ⊗ id_S`, which is injective when `Φ_M` is because `S` is flat; `Φ_N` is surjective always.

## Main results

* `Stacks.QuasiregularSequence.FlatBaseChange.solution`: Stacks tag 065L, in the form
  `RingTheory.Sequence.IsQuasiRegular.of_flat_of_isBaseChange`.

## Implementation notes

The comparison is assembled from three base-change facts about an arbitrary base change `bc : M
→ₗ[R] N` along `R → S`, none of which is in Mathlib in this form. Two of them transport a Mathlib
identification of `S ⊗[R] -` along `hbc.equiv : S ⊗[R] M ≃ₗ[S] N`, which carries the submodule
`Submodule.baseChange S P` onto the `S`-span of `bc '' P`
(`Stacks.QuasiregularSequence.FlatBaseChange.map_equiv_baseChange`):
`Stacks.QuasiregularSequence.FlatBaseChange.isBaseChange_of_submodule` transports
`Submodule.toBaseChange.toLinearEquiv`, where flatness of `S` enters, and
`Stacks.QuasiregularSequence.FlatBaseChange.isBaseChange_of_quotient` transports
`TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.tensorQuotientEquiv`, which needs no flatness. These are the two
halves of the exactness argument of the source. The third,
`Stacks.QuasiregularSequence.FlatBaseChange.injective_of_isBaseChange_square`, identifies the
bottom map of a base-changed commuting square with the top map tensored with `S`, and is where
flatness is used again.

Base change through direct sums and through `Finsupp.mapRange` is Mathlib's
`IsBaseChange.directSum` and `IsBaseChange.finsuppPow`.

The identity `Tsub M f n = J • ⊤` inside `JⁿM`
(`Stacks.QuasiregularSequence.FlatBaseChange.Tsub_eq_smul_top`) is what lets the quotient lemma be
applied to the graded pieces `JⁿM/J^{n+1}M`, as `J^{n+1}M = J · JⁿM`.

## References

* [StacksProject 065L](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/065L), Lemma 10.69.3.
-/

